- expert_quote (verbatim, greeting and sign-off removed): "Gel and acrylic manicures struggle in winter due to a combination of factors. Cold and dry air dehydrates the natural nail and makes gel and acrylic more brittle. This weakens the adhesion between the nail and enhancement material, resulting in lifting and easy breakage. Cold conditions slow the curing of acrylics. For gel manicures, however, curing speed mainly depends on the gel formula and lamp power, and ambient temperature has little influence. Winter gloves create repeated friction, and trapped moisture inside them will gradually lift the nail edge over time. To make manicures last longer through winter, you can opt for harder acrylics or use highly flexible builder gel for extensions. Daily cuticle oil keeps natural nails supple and reduces shrinkage. Many people assume faulty products are to blame, but dehydrated nails are usually the root cause of short-wearing winter manicures."
